title: Checking whether a path is watched
tags: [[Watcher]]
kind: guide
summary: mf watch check asks the live coverage whether a change at a path would be recorded — one step past eligibility — and says why not.

Eligibility says whether a path ''should'' be tracked ([[Eligibility]]); `mf watch
check` asks whether a change there ''would be recorded now'' — the live coverage. The two
can disagree:

```
mf -n docs watch check ./note.txt build/out.o /archive/x.tgz
watched      	/docs/note.txt	(changes under /docs are recorded)
NOT watched  	/build/out.o	— matches the mf_ignore pattern "(^|/)build(/.*)?$" of /
NOT watched  	/archive/x.tgz	— inside /archive (mfr_watch_exceeded — deliberate `mf watch exceeded set`, or the watch budget's frontier)
```

Paths are filesystem paths (absolute or relative to the current directory), made
repository-relative; a `/`-leading path that no longer exists is taken as already
repository-relative (an orphan's stale path). The exit code is 1 when any path is not
watched, so a script can assert on it. `--json` prints the raw answer.

The reasons, in the order they decide:

|!Reason |!Why not watched |
|`watched` |— it is |
|`untracked` |the path, or the directory covering it, is not eligible — including a file under a directory a pattern matched alone: the walk pruned the directory, so nothing can cover it |
|`excluded` |under a subtree carrying `mfr_watch_exceeded` ([[The watch budget]]), in either regime |
|`offline` |its mount point has nothing mounted ([[Offline subtrees]]) |
|`internal` |under `.metafolder/internal/`, never watched |
|`unwatched` |eligible but uncovered for no recorded reason: the kernel refused the watch (starved) — the budget regime's alone |

"Watched" for a directory is its own coverage; for anything else, its containing
directory's. The answer is the live truth, not what the fields ask for. A paused
repository answers the same — coverage stays, events buffer.

In the GUI, the metarecord list marks unwatched records in amber (the reason on hover),
and the metarecord detail panel states the verdict. A missing answer — the daemon down
or older than the endpoint — is "unknown", never "unwatched".
